New vocab for me:
- Mete: arhaic english verb that means to measure
- Mote: a tiny dry speck of dust, chaff or wood splinter. Jesus uses it here as a metaphor for a minor fault or small sin, compared to a beam which represents a major fault or significant sin
- Rend: to tear something up into pieces with great force. The opposite of sew as in sewing a garment is to rend or tear apart



- Don't cast judgement on others, for that judgement will be returned to us by others and by God
- Focus on our own major faults first before criticizing others for their minor faults
- Don't give what is holy to dogs or what is precious to swine - Be careful who we offer good counsel to. Don't offer advice to those who don't want to hear it
- Advice from Jesus to pray consistently. Those who ask shall receive, those who seek with find, to those who knock it will be opened. Our heavenly father will take care of us
- The golden rule: do unto others as you wish others would do unto you. Treat others as you wish to be treated
- Enter at the straight and narrow gate that leads to heaven rather than the wide and broad gate that leads to sin

"Enter by the narrow gate. For the gate is wide and the way is easy that leads to destruction, and those who enter by it are many.

The narrow gate has a very specific path. A difficult path. Following Christ to live a righteous life

The wide gate has many different approaches and it is an easy path that follows earthly and fleshly tempatation. This path leads to sin and destruction

- Beware of false teachers whom you will know by their fruit because good trees yield good fruit and bad trees yield bad fruit. The bad trees are cut down and thrown to the fire
- A strong message to be doers and not just hearers
- Those who hear the word of God and abide by it are like wise men who build their home on rock. When the storm comes, it will stand firm
- Those who hear the words of Christ and don't do them are like foolish men who build their home on sand. When the storm and floods come, the house will fall and perish 



Reflections:

- Take aways for me from this chapter:

1.  Avoid harsh judgement of other. We will be judged in the same measure by others and by God
2.  Look in the mirror at our own faults before we criticize others for their faults
3. Treat others as we wish to be treated. This is such a powerful principle. It's called the golden rule for a reason
4. Being righteous in a sinful world can be super difficult at times but its better to take the narrow and difficult path that leads to heaven than the easy, wide path that leads to hell
5. Be doers of the word, not just hearers of the word
6. Build your life on the bedrock that is the word of God. A solid foundation of wisdom that allows us to flourish and maintain course even when the world gets crazy
7. To the point about building on rock vs sand, Saving your time in sound money is another way of building your life on bedrock. Saving in a currency that can be controlled by men and inflated away is like building your
home on sand. When the floods come (literal flooding of money through inflation), your resources will be wiped away from inflation. Those who save in Bitcoin and Gold will protect their resources when sinful men inflate currencies
into worthlesness

